Done. Here's what was created in `/ErrorBoundary.tsx`:

**API**

| Prop | Type | Description |
|------|------|-------------|
| `children` | `ReactNode` | Content to protect |
| `fallback` | `(error, retry) => ReactNode` | Optional custom fallback UI |
| `onError` | `(error, info) => void` | Optional external error logger |

**Usage examples**

Basic — uses the built-in fallback:
```tsx
<ErrorBoundary>
  <MyComponent />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

With custom fallback UI:
```tsx
<ErrorBoundary fallback={(error, retry) => (
  <div>
    <p>{error.message}</p>
    <button onClick={retry}>Retry</button>
  </div>
)}>
  <MyComponent />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

With external logger (e.g. Sentry):
```tsx
<ErrorBoundary onError={(error, info) => Sentry.captureException(error, { extra: info })}>
  <MyComponent />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

**Key design notes:**
- `retry` resets `error` to `null`, causing a full re-render of `children` — the standard approach since error boundaries are class components (hooks can't catch render errors)
- `componentDidCatch` always logs to `console.error` regardless of whether `onError` is provided
- The default fallback shows the error message and a collapsible stack trace, keeping noise low in production while still useful in development
